  "slug": "evolutionary-rescue-adaptation-0377",
  "summary": "New experimental-evolution and genome-environment studies link haploblocks, polygenic parallelism and plasticity to whether populations are rescued or go extinct.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how genetic architecture shapes persistence under abrupt or sustained environmental change. In cowpea seed beetles, rapid, repeatable rescue on lentil relies on large non-recombining haploblocks on chromosomes 1 and 9. In outdoor Drosophila under insecticide stress, half of the populations went extinct despite strong parallel polygenic adaptation; pre-adaptation prevented extinction. Related work addresses selection across time and environments: seasonal adaptive tracking in Drosophila suzukii, pooled-sequencing of chronotype selection lines, and Boechera stricta GWAS suggesting that projected hot, dry climates may exceed adaptive capacity. Stickleback studies show that within- and transgenerational plasticity have distinct genetic architectures, and that responses to paternal predation cues differ by sex. What is new is the joint treatment of haplotype structure, contingency and plasticity as determinants of rescue outcomes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
